{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-78:37\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ is given a spatially concrete parallel in رَّبِّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَمَا بَيْنَهُمَا: lordship spans the heavens, earth, and what lies between them.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"78:37\",\"ar\":\"رَّبِّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَمَا بَيْنَهُمَا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages attach Rabb to a comprehensive domain.\",\"limits\":\"The wording does not define ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ as exactly these spatial regions.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-78:39\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The broad title Rabb al-ʿālamīn may also frame the Lord as the destination of a person's return, a relation made explicit across 78:39 and 79:44.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"78:39\",\"ar\":\"فَمَن شَآءَ ٱتَّخَذَ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِۦ مَـَٔابًا\"},{\"ref\":\"79:44\",\"ar\":\"إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ مُنتَهَىٰهَآ\"}],\"missing\":\"The shared Rabb title supports the pattern, but neither verse connects the return explicitly to the worlds named in 1:2. An ayah joining Rabb al-ʿālamīn with return or final destination could establish that link.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-79:19\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Here Rabb is the one Moses directs Pharaoh toward, with fear as the intended response; the title in 1:2 can therefore sit alongside a personal call to recognize the Lord.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"79:18\",\"ar\":\"فَقُلْ هَل لَّكَ إِلَىٰٓ أَن تَزَكَّىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"79:19\",\"ar\":\"وَأَهْدِيَكَ إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ فَتَخْشَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same title Rabb appears as the destination of guidance in a prophetic address.\",\"limits\":\"This scene does not say that the focus itself commands guidance or fear.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-79:24\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Pharaoh's claim أَنَا۠ رَبُّكُمُ ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ sharply contrasts with the focus's unqualified Rabb al-ʿālamīn: a human ruler claims lordship over his audience, then meets divine judgment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"79:24\",\"ar\":\"فَقَالَ أَنَا۠ رَبُّكُمُ ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"79:25\",\"ar\":\"فَأَخَذَهُ ٱللَّهُ نَكَالَ ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ وَٱلْأُولَىٰٓ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both formulations use Rabb; the surrounding narrative makes Pharaoh's claim an asserted rival authority.\",\"limits\":\"The passages do not explicitly quote or identify 1:2 as a rebuttal to Pharaoh.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-79:44\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ مُنتَهَىٰهَآ presents the Hour's endpoint as belonging to the Lord. Alongside Rabb al-ʿālamīn, it lets universal lordship be heard as the final horizon of events.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"79:44\",\"ar\":\"إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ مُنتَهَىٰهَآ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the Lord over all domains; this verse directs the Hour's endpoint to Rabb.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not itself mention the Hour or its endpoint.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-81:29\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The exact title رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ appears where human willing is made dependent on God's will, so universal lordship is linked to the limits of human agency.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"81:29\",\"ar\":\"وَمَا تَشَآءُونَ إِلَّآ أَن يَشَآءَ ٱللَّهُ رَبُّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared epithet directly follows the statement about whose will governs human willing.\",\"limits\":\"This parallel does not explain the precise relation between human choice and divine will.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-83:6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The exact title رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ is invoked as the one before whom all people stand, giving the focus's universal lordship an explicit scene of accountability.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"83:5\",\"ar\":\"لِيَوْمٍ عَظِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"83:6\",\"ar\":\"يَوْمَ يَقُومُ ٱلنَّاسُ لِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The identical title occurs in a day-of-standing scene for الناس.\",\"limits\":\"The focus alone does not specify standing or judgment.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-83:11\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"This verse names the Day of Recompense, a phrase also present in al-Fātiḥa 1:4; it may extend the focus's Rabb al-ʿālamīn toward the judgment that follows within the Fātiḥa's sequence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"83:11\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِينَ يُكَذِّبُونَ بِيَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The exact phrase links 83:11 to 1:4, but no wording here links the Day of Recompense back to Rabb al-ʿālamīn in 1:2. An ayah explicitly joining that title to the Day of Recompense could supply the bridge.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-83:15\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Lord of the worlds is also called their Lord when some are screened from Him; paired with 83:6, the passage places universal standing and denied access within the same judgment scene.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"83:6\",\"ar\":\"يَوْمَ يَقُومُ ٱلنَّاسُ لِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"83:15\",\"ar\":\"كَلَّآ إِنَّهُمْ عَن رَّبِّهِمْ يَوْمَئِذٍۢ لَّمَحْجُوبُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both verses name Rabb in their descriptions of that day.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not say that everyone has the same experience of standing before or being screened from the Lord.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-83:22\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-84:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The earth is described as having obeyed its Lord just before the human addressee is told of striving toward his Lord; this makes a domain within ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ an active respondent to lordship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"84:5\",\"ar\":\"وَأَذِنَتْ لِرَبِّهَا وَحُقَّتْ\"},{\"ref\":\"84:6\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّكَ كَادِحٌ إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ كَدْحًۭا فَمُلَٰقِيهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The adjacent verses move from the earth's response to the human's encounter with Rabb.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not equate the earth with all the worlds or explain how its obedience compares with human striving.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-84:23\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-85:8\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The root of ٱلْحَمْدُ recurs in ٱلْحَمِيدِ as a divine epithet in a persecution scene: the believers are targeted for faith in the Mighty, Praiseworthy God.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"85:8\",\"ar\":\"إِلَّآ أَن يُؤْمِنُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ ٱلْعَزِيزِ ٱلْحَمِيدِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's noun hamd and the epithet al-ḥamīd share the root ح م د.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not say that the persecutors praise God; it names Him as Praiseworthy while recounting their opposition.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-85:12\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Lord named in the focus is presented in another scene through the severe force of His grip, adding judgment to the range of actions associated with Rabb.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"85:11\",\"ar\":\"ذَٰلِكَ ٱلْفَوْزُ ٱلْكَبِيرُ\"},{\"ref\":\"85:12\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ بَطْشَ رَبِّكَ لَشَدِيدٌ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same title Rabb occurs after the promise of victory to believers and before further divine attributes.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not specify punishment, and this verse addresses the Prophet with a possessive form of Rabb.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-85:15\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The surrounding attribute sequence pairs the Lord's severe grip with forgiveness, affection, possession of the Throne, majesty, and effective will; it places Rabb al-ʿālamīn in a portrait of sovereign power and mercy.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"85:12\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ بَطْشَ رَبِّكَ لَشَدِيدٌ\"},{\"ref\":\"85:14\",\"ar\":\"وَهُوَ ٱلْغَفُورُ ٱلْوَدُودُ\"},{\"ref\":\"85:15\",\"ar\":\"ذُو ٱلْعَرْشِ ٱلْمَجِيدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"85:16\",\"ar\":\"فَعَّالٌۭ لِّمَا يُرِيدُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus's Rabb title meets a tightly linked sequence of divine descriptions in this passage.\",\"limits\":\"These attributes are not grammatically attached to Rabb in 1:2.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-87:1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus's declarative al-ḥamd is paralleled by a command to glorify the name of one's Lord; the two passages place praise and glorification beside the title Rabb.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"87:1\",\"ar\":\"سَبِّحِ ٱسْمَ رَبِّكَ ٱلْأَعْلَى\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages join a devotional act to a Rabb title.\",\"limits\":\"Tasbīḥ and ḥamd are distinct words and acts; this parallel does not make them synonyms.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-88:8\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-89:14\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage places Rabb at ٱلْمِرْصَادِ and then shows a person reading honor and provision as proof of his standing; this complicates any reading of lordship as benefit alone.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"89:14\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ رَبَّكَ لَبِٱلْمِرْصَادِ\"},{\"ref\":\"89:15\",\"ar\":\"فَأَكْرَمَهُۥ وَنَعَّمَهُۥ فَيَقُولُ رَبِّىٓ أَكْرَمَنِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The named Lord and the following human interpretation of provision occur in one sequence.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not mention surveillance or explain the meaning of ٱلْمِرْصَادِ.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-89:22\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Lord of the focus is presented in an end-time scene as coming with the angels in ranks, giving the broad title an eschatological manifestation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"89:22\",\"ar\":\"وَجَآءَ رَبُّكَ وَٱلْمَلَكُ صَفًّۭا صَفًّۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"89:23\",\"ar\":\"وَجِا۟ىٓءَ يَوْمَئِذٍۭ بِجَهَنَّمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Rabb title appears in a scene of the Day and its consequences.\",\"limits\":\"This wording does not settle how the coming is to be understood, and the focus gives no such scene.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-89:28\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"إِلَىٰ رَبِّكِ رَاجِعِيٓ links the Lord of all worlds with an individual soul's return, adding a personal address to the focus's universal scope.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"89:27\",\"ar\":\"يَٰٓأَيَّتُهَا ٱلنَّفْسُ ٱلْمُطْمَئِنَّةُ\"},{\"ref\":\"89:28\",\"ar\":\"ٱرْجِعِىٓ إِلَىٰ رَبِّكِ رَاضِيَةًۭ مَّرْضِيَّةًۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The broad title and the singular address both use Rabb; here the soul is told to return to Him.\",\"limits\":\"This address to the tranquil soul does not describe every individual's return in the same terms.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-92:19\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The sequence may let the focus's praise be heard as due without being repayment: the giver has no favor to repay, and acts instead in pursuit of the Lord's face.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"92:19\",\"ar\":\"وَمَا لِأَحَدٍ عِندَهُۥ مِن نِّعْمَةٍۢ تُجْزَىٰٓ\"},{\"ref\":\"92:20\",\"ar\":\"إِلَّا ٱبْتِغَآءَ وَجْهِ رَبِّهِ ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ\"}],\"missing\":\"These verses distinguish repayment from seeking the Lord, but do not mention ḥamd. A passage that explicitly joins praise to non-repayment could establish the proposed reading.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-92:20\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The Lord appears as the one whose face is sought as the motive for giving; this gives a concrete form of orientation toward the Rabb praised in the focus.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"92:18\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِى يُؤْتِى مَالَهُۥ يَتَزَكَّىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"92:20\",\"ar\":\"إِلَّا ٱبْتِغَآءَ وَجْهِ رَبِّهِ ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The action of giving is explicitly directed toward the Lord.\",\"limits\":\"This passage does not equate seeking God's face with the utterance of praise in 1:2.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-93:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Here Rabb names a bond of non-abandonment: the focus's broad lordship can be heard alongside continuing care for a particular addressee.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"93:3\",\"ar\":\"مَا وَدَّعَكَ رَبُّكَ وَمَا قَلَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"Rabb is the subject of a direct reassurance about the addressee's relation to God.\",\"limits\":\"The assurance is addressed to the Prophet and is not stated in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-93:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"وَلَسَوْفَ يُعْطِيكَ رَبُّكَ presents the Lord as a future giver whose giving reaches the addressee's satisfaction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"93:5\",\"ar\":\"وَلَسَوْفَ يُعْطِيكَ رَبُّكَ فَتَرْضَىٰٓ\"},{\"ref\":\"93:6\",\"ar\":\"أَلَمْ يَجِدْكَ يَتِيمًۭا فَـَٔاوَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The passage connects Rabb with a promised gift and recalls earlier care.\",\"limits\":\"This promise to one addressee does not specify what ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ means in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-93:11\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The favor is attributed to Rabb and then made the subject of public speech, an explicit bridge from lordship to voiced acknowledgment akin to al-ḥamd.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"93:11\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا بِنِعْمَةِ رَبِّكَ فَحَدِّثْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse joins Rabb's favor to a command to speak of it.\",\"limits\":\"It does not use the word al-ḥamd or say every mention takes the same form.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-94:8\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"وَإِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ فَٱرْغَب directs desire toward the same Lord to whom the focus assigns praise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"94:7\",\"ar\":\"فَإِذَا فَرَغْتَ فَٱنصَبْ\"},{\"ref\":\"94:8\",\"ar\":\"وَإِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ فَٱرْغَب\"}],\"activation\":\"The sequence of effort and turning desire ends with a direct Rabb address.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not express desire or prescribe this action.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-95:8\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"After naming the Day of Recompense, this passage asks whether Allah is the best of judges; it may connect the focus's divine lordship to judgment, as the Fātiḥa later names the Owner of that Day.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"95:7\",\"ar\":\"فَمَا يُكَذِّبُكَ بَعْدُ بِٱلدِّينِ\"},{\"ref\":\"95:8\",\"ar\":\"أَلَيْسَ ٱللَّهُ بِأَحْكَمِ ٱلْحَٰكِمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The passages link Allah to judgment, but this item does not connect that role directly to Rabb al-ʿālamīn in 1:2. An ayah joining the Rabb title with judgment could supply the link.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-96:1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The title Rabb is linked directly to creating in ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ; this gives the focus's lordship a concrete creative relation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"96:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱقْرَأْ بِٱسْمِ رَبِّكَ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ\"},{\"ref\":\"96:2\",\"ar\":\"خَلَقَ ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ مِنْ عَلَقٍ\"}],\"activation\":\"The relative clause identifies the addressed Lord by the act of creation.\",\"limits\":\"The focus itself does not state how creation relates to lordship.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-96:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Lord is explicitly called ٱلْأَكْرَمُ, adding generosity to the Rabb title that the focus places at the head of the worlds.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"96:3\",\"ar\":\"وَرَبُّكَ ٱلْأَكْرَمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse predicates a quality directly of Rabb.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not name this quality or a particular recipient of it.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-96:4\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The relative clause following Rabb al-akram identifies teaching by the pen as a divine act, giving lordship an instructional dimension.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"96:3\",\"ar\":\"وَرَبُّكَ ٱلْأَكْرَمُ\"},{\"ref\":\"96:4\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِى عَلَّمَ بِٱلْقَلَمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The relative clause grammatically continues the description of Rabb in 96:3.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not mention teaching or the pen.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-97:4\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The angels and the Spirit descend by their Lord's permission for every affair; this extends the Rabb relation to unseen agents and the ordering of affairs.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"97:4\",\"ar\":\"تَنَزَّلُ ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ وَٱلرُّوحُ فِيهَا بِإِذْنِ رَبِّهِم مِّن كُلِّ أَمْرٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The plural possessive رَبِّهِم governs the angels' descent and its scope.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not state that ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ specifically refers to angels or these affairs.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-99:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The earth receives its Lord's inspiration and reports its news; a domain within the focus's worlds is shown responding to divine direction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"99:4\",\"ar\":\"يَوْمَئِذٍۢ تُحَدِّثُ أَخْبَارَهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"99:5\",\"ar\":\"بِأَنَّ رَبَّكَ أَوْحَىٰ لَهَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The earth's disclosure is explained by what its Lord revealed to it.\",\"limits\":\"This scene does not establish that all domains named in ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ speak or receive inspiration.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-100:3\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-100:4\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-100:6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ لِرَبِّهِۦ لَكَنُودٌۭ gives a negative counterpart to the focus: praise is assigned to God, while the human is described as ungrateful toward his Lord.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"100:6\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ لِرَبِّهِۦ لَكَنُودٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse names the human's response to his Lord in terms opposite to grateful acknowledgment.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not present كَنُود as a technical antonym of حَمْد.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-100:11\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"After the passage exposes ingratitude and what is hidden in hearts, it says their Lord is fully aware of them on that day; the Rabb relation includes knowledge of inward response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"100:6\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ لِرَبِّهِۦ لَكَنُودٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"100:10\",\"ar\":\"وَحُصِّلَ مَا فِى ٱلصُّدُورِ\"},{\"ref\":\"100:11\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ رَبَّهُم بِهِمْ يَوْمَئِذٍۢ لَّخَبِيرٌۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same passage moves from ingratitude to disclosure of hearts and the Lord's knowledge.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not specify this day or the Lord's knowledge of hidden deeds.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-102:3\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-102:4\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:R-102:3\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-106:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"رَبَّ هَٰذَا ٱلْبَيْتِ qualifies the Rabb title by a particular sanctuary; the Lord of all worlds is also addressed through a specific communal place of worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"106:3\",\"ar\":\"فَلْيَعْبُدُوا۟ رَبَّ هَٰذَا ٱلْبَيْتِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages use Rabb, while this one attaches it to the House and commands worship.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not say the House exhausts or defines the worlds named in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-106:4\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Food and security are named as gifts of the Lord of the House and as the context for worship; these concrete benefits give the focus's praise and lordship an experiential setting.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"106:3\",\"ar\":\"فَلْيَعْبُدُوا۟ رَبَّ هَٰذَا ٱلْبَيْتِ\"},{\"ref\":\"106:4\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِىٓ أَطْعَمَهُم مِّن جُوعٍۢ وَءَامَنَهُم مِّنْ خَوْفٍۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The relative clause explains the Lord's relation to the Quraysh through feeding and safety.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not explicitly call these benefits praise or use the word al-ḥamd.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-108:2\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Prayer and sacrifice are directed to one's Lord after the mention of abundant gift, extending the focus's praise-to-Rabb relation into acts of worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"108:1\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّآ أَعْطَيْنَٰكَ ٱلْكَوْثَرَ\"},{\"ref\":\"108:2\",\"ar\":\"فَصَلِّ لِرَبِّكَ وَٱنْحَرْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Lord is the recipient of commanded acts in a context of divine giving.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not identify prayer or sacrifice with the noun al-ḥamd.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-109:1\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-109:2\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-109:3\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-109:4\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-109:5\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:R-109:3\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-109:6\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-110:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"فَسَبِّحْ بِحَمْدِ رَبِّكَ gathers the focus's hamd and Rabb in a command: the foundational praise becomes an instructed response after people enter God's religion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"110:2\",\"ar\":\"وَرَأَيْتَ ٱلنَّاسَ يَدْخُلُونَ فِى دِينِ ٱللَّهِ أَفْوَاجًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"110:3\",\"ar\":\"فَسَبِّحْ بِحَمْدِ رَبِّكَ وَٱسْتَغْفِرْهُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse explicitly joins ḥamd and Rabb, the two terms central to the focus.\",\"limits\":\"The later command adds tasbīḥ and seeking forgiveness, which are not stated in 1:2.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-112:2\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-114:1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"رَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ narrows the focus's broad Rabb al-ʿālamīn to humanity and presents lordship as a source of refuge; the following titles add kingship and divinity over the same people.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"114:1\",\"ar\":\"قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ\"},{\"ref\":\"114:2\",\"ar\":\"مَلِكِ ٱلنَّاسِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated Rabb title is given a human domain and a protective function in the refuge formula.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not say that people exhaust ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The exact title Rabb al-ʿālamīn occurs in a statement limiting human willing by God's will and in a scene of all people standing before Him; together these make the focus's universal lordship connect dependence with accountability.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"81:29\",\"ar\":\"وَمَا تَشَآءُونَ إِلَّآ أَن يَشَآءَ ٱللَّهُ رَبُّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"83:6\",\"ar\":\"يَوْمَ يَقُومُ ٱلنَّاسُ لِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same full epithet appears in both passages, but under distinct conditions: human willing and final standing.\",\"limits\":\"The shared title does not itself state a causal link between willing and the day of standing.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In 84:5 and 99:5, the earth obeys its Lord and later reports its news by His direction; read together, these scenes make a domain of the worlds an actor in divine governance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"84:5\",\"ar\":\"وَأَذِنَتْ لِرَبِّهَا وَحُقَّتْ\"},{\"ref\":\"99:4\",\"ar\":\"يَوْمَئِذٍۢ تُحَدِّثُ أَخْبَارَهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"99:5\",\"ar\":\"بِأَنَّ رَبَّكَ أَوْحَىٰ لَهَا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both scenes identify the earth's response in relation to its Lord, offering a concrete domain beneath the focus's plural worlds.\",\"limits\":\"The passages depict different scenes and do not establish that all worlds act like the earth.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
